  {
   "title": "Figure 5-16's caption and its axis labels both contradict the step",
   "text": "§5-11 step 1 tells you to connect the oscilloscope to pin 16 of U6 with the scope common on pin 13, set to 5 V/div at 10 us/div, and compare against Figure 5-16. The figure is captioned 'Waveform at Pin 13 of U6' -- pin 13 is the common, not the signal -- and its artwork is annotated 1 V and 10 ms, which is neither the vertical nor the horizontal scale the step asks for. Follow the step's text and its scope settings; use the figure for the shape of the waveform only.",
   "refs": [
    "U6"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Fault code 3324 prints the same test as 3321",
   "text": "3321 (10M Ohm Ratio Fault) and 3324 (100M Ohm Ratio Fault) are printed with identical text in §5-2: 'the 2/5/10V SOURCE circuit on the Ohms Cal assembly is set to 10V which is routed to the Ohms Main assembly and connected to the 10 MΩ string. The resulting 1V across the 1 MΩ portion is measured for 1V ±3% ±15 mV.' By the pattern every other ratio code follows -- each value against the decade below it -- 3324 should read the 100 Mohm string and the drop across its 10 Mohm portion. The code's own title and the 3317-3324 sequence say the same. This dataset carries the text as printed and flags it here rather than correcting it.",
   "refs": []
  },
  {
   "title": "The 1.9 ohm check code's band is dominated by its floor",
   "text": "Fault code 3334 expects 24.7 mV ±3 % ±15 mV. Three per cent of 24.7 mV is 0.74 mV, so the ±15 mV floor is more than twenty times the proportional term and the code passes anything from about 9 mV to about 40 mV. It is a continuity test on K4, K5, K30 and R42, not a calibration test, and a 1.9 ohm value that is several per cent out will pass it. §5-10 step 2's four-terminal measurement at ±0.1 % is the check that would catch that, and it is not run by the diagnostics.",
   "refs": [
    "R42",
    "K4",
    "K5",
    "K30"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Every relay on this board is latching, and holds state with the power off",
   "text": "All 31 relays are latching armature types, and §5-10 step 1 turns that into the board's main troubleshooting technique: set the calibrator to the failing function, switch it off, and the relays stay where they were, so the path can be traced cold. It also means a board pulled after a fault comes out in the failing configuration and will still be in it when it goes back. Individual relays are checked by putting an external supply -- the manual suggests a 9 V battery -- across the set coil or the reset coil. Sheet 1's bottom-view keys give the pinouts (4FCL: 7,5,3,1,2,4,6,8 over 10,12,14,16,15,13,11,9; 2FCL: 1,2,4,6,8 over 16,15,13,11,9) and sheet 4 shows every coil with its set and reset windings. These are 5 V coils designed for millisecond latching pulses; pulse them briefly.",
   "refs": []
  },
  {
   "title": "§2-174's 'two latch/driver ICs at a time' is true of two groups out of three",
   "text": "§2-174 says decoder U13 enables two latch/driver ICs at a time. There are seven drivers on A9, so they cannot pair evenly, and sheet 4 shows what actually happens: U13's OUT5 enables U15 and U16, OUT6 enables U18 and U20, and OUT7 enables three -- U14, U17 and U19. The other five outputs are not connected. An enable line found pulling three chips is correct rather than a short.",
   "refs": [
    "U13",
    "U14",
    "U15",
    "U16",
    "U17",
    "U18",
    "U19",
    "U20"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "The 0.22 uF driver decoupling capacitors read as 22 uF at low resolution",
   "text": "Each UCN5801 has its own decoupling capacitor between +5 RLH and RLH COM at its COM pin -- C39 to C43 and C45, part of the fifteen-position 0.22 uF polyester row in Table 6-10. Sheet 4 prints them as '.22uF', and at the resolution of the scan the leading decimal point is easy to lose. There is no 22 uF part anywhere on A9; the largest capacitor on the board is C36 at 47 uF and the next is C19/C24/C25 at 10 uF.",
   "refs": [
    "C39",
    "C40",
    "C41",
    "C42",
    "C43",
    "C45"
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Three test points share one printed name",
   "text": "TP16, TP17 and TP18 all read '2 WIRE COMP. CIRCUIT' in the sheet 1 legend, so the legend alone cannot tell them apart. §5-11 step 2 is what disambiguates them: TP18 to TP17 spans the 500 ohm resistor in Z2 and TP16 to TP6 spans the 1 kohm. The legend's own zone references separate TP16 (PG3 B3) from TP17 and TP18 (both PG3 B5). The signal names carried in this dataset add that distinction so the three are not interchangeable in search.",
   "refs": [
    "TP16",
    "TP17",
    "TP18",
    "Z2"
   ]
  }
